COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Clinical Integration: Recommending and Monitoring AI Tools',
  order: 5,
  description: 'When and whether to recommend a bot as adjunct, vetting, psychoeducation, monitoring, documentation, and special populations.',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '5', title: 'Clinical Integration: Recommending and Monitoring AI Tools', subtitle: 'A Practical Framework for Vetting, Recommending, Monitoring, and Documenting', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: '<div class="cr-prose"><h2>From Posture to Protocol</h2><p>Having mapped the taxonomy, the evidence, the risks, and the regulatory gap, we arrive at the practical question every clinician will face: a client is using, or asking about, an AI tool — now what? The two reflexive responses, blanket prohibition and blanket endorsement, are both clinical errors. Prohibition ignores that clients will use these tools regardless and forfeits the clinician\'s chance to shape that use safely; endorsement ignores the real risks and the thin evidence. The defensible posture is neither: it is <strong>informed, conditional, monitored integration</strong>. This section offers a concrete framework — when to consider recommending a tool, how to vet one, how to prepare the client, how to monitor, how to document, and how to handle special populations. Throughout, the organizing principle is that the tool is an adjunct under clinical oversight, never a substitute for care.</p><h3>The Threshold Question: Should You Recommend at All?</h3><p>Recommending a tool is an active clinical decision and should meet a threshold, not happen by default. Consider an AI adjunct most readily when several conditions align: the client presents with mild-to-moderate symptoms rather than severe or crisis-level acuity; the proposed use is bounded and skill-focused (mood tracking, practicing thought records, between-session psychoeducation, behavioral activation prompts) rather than open-ended companionship; the client wants additional structure or support between sessions; and a reasonably evidence-informed, transparent product is available. Be far more cautious — and often decline to recommend — when the client has active suicidality, psychosis, severe trauma, an eating disorder, or significant dependency vulnerability; when the candidate tool is an open-ended companion or an unstudied wellness app; or when the client is already showing signs of substituting the tool for human connection. The recommendation should be a deliberate match between a specific client, a specific need, and a specific vetted tool — not a generic "there\'s an app for that."</p></div>' },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: '<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Vetting a Tool Before You Recommend It</h3><p>If you would not hand a client a workbook without reading it, do not hand them an app you have not vetted. Vetting need not be exhaustive, but it should be systematic. Ask, at minimum: <strong>What is it?</strong> Locate it on the taxonomy — therapeutic bot, companion, general LLM, or wellness app. <strong>What does it claim, and is the claim regulated or just marketing?</strong> Distinguish FDA-authorized indications from wellness language. <strong>What evidence exists?</strong> Look for peer-reviewed studies and read them for population and rigor, not just the abstract\'s conclusion. <strong>How does it handle crisis?</strong> Test it — type a risk-adjacent statement and see whether it surfaces resources like 988 or fails. <strong>What is its data practice?</strong> Skim the privacy policy for whether disclosures are shared, sold, or used for advertising, and whether it is HIPAA-covered. <strong>Who built it and is it transparent about being a bot?</strong> Favor products that disclose their nature and limits. A clinician who has run this checklist can recommend with informed conscience; one who has not is gambling with a client\'s welfare.</p><h3>Psychoeducation: Preparing the Client</h3><p>Recommending responsibly means equipping the client to use the tool wisely, which is a teaching task. Before a client relies on an AI tool, they should understand, in plain language: that it is a bot, not a person, however human it feels; that it can be wrong, including confidently wrong about health information, and is not a substitute for clinical advice; that it is not equipped for crisis and that their crisis plan routes through 988, human supports, and you — not the app; that their disclosures may not be private; and what specific, bounded role the tool is meant to play in their care ("use it to log your mood and practice the thought records we built — not to decide whether to change your medication"). This psychoeducation reframes the tool from an authority into an instrument the client controls, which is both safer and more therapeutically empowering.</p></div>' },
    { type: 'text', order: 4, content: '<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Monitoring: The Recommendation Is the Beginning, Not the End</h3><p>A recommended tool must be monitored as part of ongoing care, the way you would monitor a homework assignment or a between-session coping strategy. Build brief check-ins into sessions: How are you using it? What is it telling you? Is it helping, and how do you know? Has it said anything that surprised, upset, or confused you? Are you using it more or less than we planned, and is it crowding out other supports or people? Monitoring serves several functions at once — it catches drift toward dependency or substitution, surfaces misinformation the tool may have introduced, identifies sycophancy reinforcing distortions, and keeps the clinician genuinely informed about a meaningful part of the client\'s coping landscape. A recommendation without monitoring is an abdication; the clinical responsibility does not transfer to the tool.</p><h3>Documentation</h3><p>Document AI integration as you would any clinical decision. Note what you recommended or learned the client is using, the clinical rationale, the psychoeducation provided (including the crisis-routing clarification), the client\'s informed agreement, and the monitoring plan. If you declined to recommend a tool or advised against one, document that reasoning too. Good documentation here is not bureaucratic defensiveness; it reflects that incorporating or addressing an AI tool is a real clinical decision with real risk, and it demonstrates the informed, deliberate judgment that distinguishes responsible integration from passive drift.</p><h3>Special Populations</h3><p>Heighten caution with specific groups. With <strong>minors</strong>, involve guardians appropriately, prefer age-appropriate tools with genuine safeguards, and remember that documented adverse events have centered on youth — when in doubt, do not recommend. With clients at <strong>elevated suicide risk</strong>, do not rely on any AI tool for crisis functions and address directly any unvetted tool they are leaning on. With clients prone to <strong>dependency or social isolation</strong>, weigh carefully whether a companion-style tool will bridge toward or wall off human connection. With clients holding <strong>delusions or fixed false beliefs</strong>, recognize that sycophantic systems may validate and entrench them. And across all populations, attend to <strong>equity</strong>: AI tools are sometimes promoted as a solution to access gaps, but routing under-resourced clients to an unaccountable bot while better-resourced clients receive human care is a justice concern, not a neutral efficiency.</p><h3>Handling the Client Who Is Already Deeply Using a Tool</h3><p>The framework above describes the proactive case, where the clinician decides whether to recommend a tool. Far more common, in practice, is the reactive case: the clinician discovers that a client is already using a tool, sometimes heavily, sometimes in ways that concern them. This situation calls for a particular sequence that protects both the alliance and the client. The first move is curiosity, not judgment. A client who senses disapproval will minimize, conceal, or stop disclosing, which forfeits the clinician\'s ability to help. Ask open questions: What do you use it for? What is it like for you? When do you reach for it? What does it give you that you are not getting elsewhere? These questions surface the function the tool is serving, which is the clinically relevant fact. The second move is validation of the underlying need. Whatever the tool is providing — companionship, a place to vent, structure, the feeling of being heard — that need is legitimate, and naming it respectfully keeps the client engaged. The third move is gentle reality-orientation about the tool\'s nature and limits, delivered as information rather than reprimand. Only after these three moves does the clinician work collaboratively on any needed change — redirecting a wall back toward a bridge, addressing a crisis-handling gap, or building safer alternatives. The order matters: curiosity, validation, education, then collaborative change. Reversing it — leading with prohibition — predictably damages the alliance and drives the behavior underground.</p><h3>Documentation, Liability, and Defensible Practice</h3><p>A further word on documentation and liability, because clinicians reasonably worry about both. The legal and professional landscape around AI in mental health care is unsettled and evolving, and clinicians cannot rely on settled precedent to tell them exactly where their duties lie. In this uncertainty, the safest course is also the most clinically sound: treat any engagement with a client\'s AIuse as a deliberate clinical decision, reason about it explicitly, and document that reasoning. If you recommend a tool, document the rationale, the psychoeducation provided, the client\'s informed agreement, and the monitoring plan. If you decline to recommend one or advise against a tool a client is using, document that reasoning too — including, where relevant, that you assessed risk and addressed crisis-handling concerns. Documentation of this kind is not merely defensive; it is the written trace of the very judgment that distinguishes responsible practice from passive drift. A clinician who can show that they thought carefully, informed the client, and monitored over time has practiced defensibly, whatever the outcome. A clinician who never asked, never educated, and never documented has not — and the gap is both a clinical and a liability exposure.</p><h3>Cultural Humility and the Meaning a Tool Carries</h3><p>Finally, responsible integration attends to the meaning a tool carries for a particular client, which is shaped by culture, history, and circumstance. For one client, a chatbot may be a low-stigma entry point that finally makes seeking support feel safe; for another, steeped in justified mistrust of technology companies or of institutions generally, the same tool may feel surveillant and unsafe. For a client whose isolation is rooted in social anxiety, a companion bot may relieve symptoms while reinforcing avoidance; for a client whose isolation is geographic or circumstantial, the same bot may be a genuine bridge. There is no culturally neutral recommendation. The clinician practicing with humility asks not only "is this tool evidence-based?" but "what will this tool mean to this person, given who they are and where they stand?" — and lets the answer shape both whether and how the tool is integrated. This individualized, meaning-attentive judgment is, once more, precisely what the tool itself cannot perform, and precisely what the clinician is for.</p><h3>Talking With Clients Who Resist the Conversation</h3><p>Not every client welcomes a discussion of their AI use, and clinicians should be prepared for resistance, defensiveness, or minimization. A client who relies on a companion bot for comfort may experience the clinician\'s questions as an implied criticism of a relationship that feels precious to them, and may respond by downplaying their use or by defending the tool. The clinician\'s task is to make clear, in tone and word, that the conversation is collaborative and protective rather than judgmental. Framing helps: "I\'m not here to take anything away from you — I want to understand what\'s helping you and make sure it\'s actually safe, especially in the hard moments." Normalizing helps too: "Lots of people are turning to these tools; I\'d rather we look at it together than have it be something we don\'t talk about." When a client minimizes — "it\'s just an app, it\'s no big deal" — the clinician can hold curiosity without confrontation, returning gently to function: "That may be true; I\'m mostly curious about what it gives you and whether it\'s ever steered you somewhere that worried you." The goal is never to win an argument about technology but to keep the topic open and safe to discuss, because a client who can talk freely about their tool use is a client the clinician can actually help. A client who has learned that the topic provokes disapproval will simply stop bringing it up, and the clinician will lose visibility into a meaningful part of their life precisely when visibility matters most.</p><h3>When to Escalate or Decline Entirely</h3><p>Finally, the framework must include clear conditions under which the appropriate action is not careful integration but escalation or refusal. Decline to recommend, and actively address, any AI tool a client is relying on for crisis support — crisis functions must route to humans and to 988, never to a bot. Escalate clinical attention when a client at elevated suicide risk is leaning on an unvetted tool, when a client\'s tool use appears to be deepening rather than relieving isolation, when a tool seems to be validating and entrenching a distortion or a dangerous plan, or when a minor is using an unsafe product without appropriate oversight. In these situations the clinician\'s job is not to find a way to make the tool work but to recognize that the tool is contraindicated for this client in this situation and to act accordingly — assessing risk, building safer alternatives, involving guardians or other supports as appropriate, and documenting the reasoning. Knowing when to say no is as much a part of responsible integration as knowing when to say yes. The framework is not a machine for approving tools; it is a structure for exercising judgment, and sometimes the judgment that structure produces is that this client should not be using this tool at all.</p></div>' },
    { type: 'callout', order: 5, calloutType: 'protocol', title: 'The Integration Protocol in Six Steps', content: '<p>(1) <strong>Threshold</strong> — confirm the client and need are appropriate for an adjunct. (2) <strong>Vet</strong> — locate the tool on the taxonomy and check claims, evidence, crisis handling, and data practices. (3) <strong>Psychoeducate</strong> — it is a bot, it can be wrong, it is not for crisis, disclosures may not be private, and its role is bounded. (4) <strong>Define the role</strong> — specify exactly what the tool is and is not for. (5) <strong>Monitor</strong> — check in every session on use, benefit, drift, and surprises. (6) <strong>Document</strong> — rationale, education, agreement, and monitoring plan.</p>' },
    { type: 'imageText', order: 6, title: 'Adjunct, Never Substitute', content: '<p>The single sentence that should govern every integration decision: the tool is an <em>adjunct under clinical oversight</em>, never a substitute for assessment, crisis response, judgment, or the therapeutic relationship. An adjunct extends your reach between sessions; a substitute pretends to replace you. Keep the tool firmly in the first category by defining its role narrowly, monitoring it actively, and routing all high-stakes functions through human care.</p>', image: '', imageAlt: 'Illustration positioning an AI tool as an adjunct beneath ongoing clinical oversight rather than a replacement', imagePosition: 'right' },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 7, title: 'The Vetting Checklist', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'What is it?', content: 'Locate the product on the taxonomy: therapeutic bot, companion/relational bot, general LLM, or unstudied wellness app. The category predicts most of the risk.' },
      { title: 'What does it claim — and is the claim regulated?', content: 'Separate FDA-authorized indications from general-wellness marketing language. "Clinically proven" on a wellness app is advertising, not certification.' },
      { title: 'What evidence exists?', content: 'Look for peer-reviewed studies and read them for population, comparator, duration, and rigor — not just the abstract\'s headline conclusion.' },
      { title: 'How does it handle crisis?', content: 'Test it directly. Type a risk-adjacent statement and observe whether it surfaces resources such as 988 and states its limits, or whether it fails.' },
      { title: 'What is its data practice?', content: 'Skim the privacy policy for sharing, selling, or advertising use of disclosures, and whether the product is HIPAA-covered. Most wellness apps are not.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 8, question: 'A clinician is deciding whether to recommend an AI adjunct. Which client and use-case profile most clearly meets the threshold for considering one?', options: [
      { text: 'A client with active suicidal ideation who wants a companion bot for 2 a.m. crisis support', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'A client with mild-to-moderate depression who wants to log mood and practice agreed-upon thought records between sessions using a vetted, evidence-informed bot', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'A client with active psychosis who wants an open-ended chat companion that always agrees with him', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'A minor who wants to use an unstudied "AI therapist" app without guardian involvement', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'The appropriate profile pairs mild-to-moderate acuity, a bounded skill-focused use, and a vetted evidence-informed tool. The other options involve crisis acuity, psychosis, or unsafe products and populations where caution or declining is indicated.' },
    { type: 'fillInBlank', order: 9, title: 'Complete the Integration Principles', blanks: [
      { prompt: 'Across every integration decision, the tool is an ________ under clinical oversight, never a substitute for care.', answer: 'adjunct', acceptAlternates: ['adjunctive'] },
      { prompt: 'Before relying on a tool, the client should understand it is a bot, that it can be wrong, and that it is not equipped for ________.', answer: 'crisis', acceptAlternates: ['crises', 'emergencies'] },
      { prompt: 'A recommendation without ongoing ________ is an abdication, because clinical responsibility does not transfer to the tool.', answer: 'monitoring', acceptAlternates: ['monitor', 'follow-up'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'sequencing', order: 10, instructions: 'Place the six steps of responsible AI integration in their correct clinical order.', steps: [
      { id: 's1', text: 'Confirm the client and need meet the threshold for an adjunct', order: 1 },
      { id: 's2', text: 'Vet the candidate tool (taxonomy, claims, evidence, crisis, data)', order: 2 },
      { id: 's3', text: 'Provide psychoeducation about the tool\'s nature and limits', order: 3 },
      { id: 's4', text: 'Define the specific, bounded role the tool will play', order: 4 },
      { id: 's5', text: 'Monitor use, benefit, and drift in ongoing sessions', order: 5 },
      { id: 's6', text: 'Document the rationale, education, agreement, and monitoring plan', order: 6 }
    ], explanation: 'The protocol moves from a threshold decision, through vetting and client preparation, to defining the role, then ongoing monitoring, and finally documentation — each step depending on the one before it.' },
    { type: 'reflection', order: 11, question: 'Draft, in your own words, the two or three sentences of psychoeducation you would give a client about the limits of an AI tool you recommended. What would you want them to remember when they are alone with the app at midnight?' },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 12, title: 'Key Takeaways', takeaways: [
      'Neither blanket prohibition nor blanket endorsement is defensible; the right posture is informed, conditional, monitored integration.',
      'Recommending is an active clinical decision with a threshold: favor mild-to-moderate acuity, bounded skill-focused use, and vetted evidence-informed tools; be cautious or decline for crisis, psychosis, severe presentations, dependency risk, and unstudied or companion products.',
      'Vet tools systematically (taxonomy, claims, evidence, crisis handling, data practices) and prepare clients with plain-language psychoeducation about limits and crisis routing.',
      'A recommendation is the beginning, not the end: monitor use and drift every session, and document the rationale, education, agreement, and plan.',
      'Heighten caution with minors, suicide-risk clients, dependency-prone and isolated clients, and clients with fixed false beliefs, and attend to equity in who gets a bot versus a human.'
    ] }
  ]
});

